00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:08,260
You don't want to give up like this.

262
00:15:08,380 --> 00:15:09,680
Why shouldn't I, huh, Doc?

263
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:11,800
You tell me, why shouldn't I give up?

264
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:16,180
I mean, if I can't make it as Santa Claus with a bunch of eight -year -old orphans,

265
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:19,040
how am I supposed to make it as Walter Bodewell?

266
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:20,960
Well, you work at it, Walter.

267
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:22,920
You chip away at each problem.

268
00:15:23,240 --> 00:15:27,940
You speak from the heart, occasionally without a prepared statement.

269
00:15:28,420 --> 00:15:32,620
And then when you listen back, you know, you start to sound just like everybody

270
00:15:32,620 --> 00:15:34,480
else. You're human, Walter.

271
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:38,320
That means occasionally you're gonna have some pain and sometimes you're gonna feel

272
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:42,860
lonely. But if you just keep reaching out, there's gonna be somebody there to lend

273
00:15:42,860 --> 00:15:44,180
you a hand.

274
00:15:49,980 --> 00:15:51,980
Thanks, Doc. Goodbye,

275
00:15:52,640 --> 00:15:56,760
Walter. Oh, that's it.

276
00:15:56,820 --> 00:15:57,940
That's it. What's it?

277
00:15:57,940 --> 00:15:58,660
I see it now.

278
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:00,320
Yeah, this is a good idea, Walter.

279
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:01,960
I'm gonna kill myself, too.

280
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:06,740
That's it. You just have a problem, and you jump down the chimney, Walter.

281
00:16:06,780 --> 00:16:07,920
No, you can't do that.

282
00:16:07,980 --> 00:16:08,660
Why not, Walter?

283
00:16:08,660 --> 00:16:09,460
It's my chimney.

284
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:11,480
What are you talking about?

285
00:16:12,960 --> 00:16:15,260
Walter, you think you're the only one with problems?

286
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:19,160
Well, I got a problem, too, Walter, because I got a big guy on my roof.

287
00:16:19,320 --> 00:16:23,340
I've been counseling for four years, and he's threatening to kill himself because I

288
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:24,340
can't talk him out of it.

289
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:26,080
I can't live without my conscience, Walter.

290
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:28,540
No! No! Excuse me.

291
00:16:28,920 --> 00:16:30,000
You mind if I go first?

292
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:31,360
Doc, I'm not gonna let you do this.

293
00:16:31,480 --> 00:16:32,300
You have a wife.

294
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:33,340
You have children.

295
00:16:33,640 --> 00:16:36,000
Yeah, their Christmas is pretty much shot as it is.

296
00:16:36,560 --> 00:16:38,080
You are crazy.

297
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:39,200
Don't try to stop me, Walter.

298
00:16:39,340 --> 00:16:41,160
Doc, I can't let you do this!

299
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:45,880
Doc, quit whining.

300
00:16:45,980 --> 00:16:47,340
It's really pathetic.

301
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:48,900
It's not me.

302
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:51,180
Well, what is it?

303
00:17:05,240 --> 00:17:09,760
Oh, yeah. So you bought a poppy for your kid for Christmas?

304
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:15,720
Mm -hmm. He's been asking for one for years, so I guess he found it a little early.

305
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:21,160
Yeah. What's the matter?

306
00:17:21,160 --> 00:17:22,600
What kind of puppy is it?

307
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:26,480
Brown! And what is he doing?

308
00:17:26,820 --> 00:17:28,260
Look at my face!

309
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:31,820
Ben, what are you...

310
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:33,320
I used to have a dog.

311
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:35,460
I had a German Shepherd.

312
00:17:37,860 --> 00:17:39,460
Was he a nice dog?

313
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:46,060
Oh, yeah. He was the only guy who didn't get uncomfortable when he was around me.

314
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:49,700
I remember we used to have this great game we played together.

315
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:51,760
It was called...

316
00:17:54,820 --> 00:17:57,760
You mean you'd throw the ball and he'd go fetch it?

317
00:17:58,100 --> 00:17:59,580
That would've worked, too.

318
00:18:01,500 --> 00:18:03,100
What happened to him?

319
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:10,420
He died... 17 years ago.

320
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:13,000
He was the last person I ever lived with.

321
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:16,020
Were you sad?

322
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:20,960
Yeah. Did you cry?

323
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:29,780
Yes. Are you still gonna kill yourself?

324
00:18:29,940 --> 00:18:32,640
Ben. I don't know.

325
00:18:34,600 --> 00:18:39,000
Well, if you don't kill yourself, you can have my puppy.

326
00:18:41,020 --> 00:18:43,520
Hello? Mr. Bobwell?

327
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:47,100
Mom, what happened to him?

328
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:49,080
I don't know, honey.

329
00:18:50,060 --> 00:18:53,260
Mr. Bodewell? Dad?

330
00:18:54,040 --> 00:18:59,560
Jason? Jason? Yes?

331
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:05,500
Dad? Mr. Bodewell?

332
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:06,860
Jason, are you OK?

333
00:19:07,720 --> 00:19:09,020
Yeah, I think we're OK.

334
00:19:09,400 --> 00:19:10,580
This is my puppy.

335
00:19:11,440 --> 00:19:12,820
He's a nice one.

336
00:19:17,900 --> 00:19:30,340
Merry Christmas. Ho, ho, ho!

337
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:36,280
Ho! Oh, and now it's time for the opening of the Siever family Christmas Eve

338
00:19:36,280 --> 00:19:41,340
presents. Yay! Oh, now, remember, I didn't buy any of these presents, so if you

339
00:19:41,340 --> 00:19:43,020
don't like your present, it's not my fault.

340
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:45,840
Walter. Oh, I'm sorry.

341
00:19:45,980 --> 00:19:47,440
Oh, here. Ho, ho, ho!

342
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:51,040
Better, better. Okay, this one's for Carol.

343
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:52,600
This one's for Mike.

344
00:19:53,000 --> 00:20:01,020
Oh. Dear Mike, this has been handed down in our family for three generations to each

345
00:20:01,020 --> 00:20:02,800
man as he comes of age.

346
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:08,660
Oh. Oh. A razor.

347
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:12,100
Uh, I think we better switch.

348
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:13,820
What, and break family tradition?

349
00:20:20,080 --> 00:20:21,460
Oh, thank you.

350
00:20:22,300 --> 00:20:24,460
Oh, thank you, thank you.

351
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:29,420
All right. The most improved cook of the year.

352
00:20:31,680 --> 00:20:33,920
Does that mean y 'all want me to make Christmas dinner?

353
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:38,100
Uh, no. Thanks anyway.

354
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:39,280
We love you.

355
00:20:39,980 --> 00:20:43,960
Is this the exciting and romantic gift I've been waiting all day for?

356
00:20:44,020 --> 00:20:45,080
That's the one.

357
00:20:45,660 --> 00:20:46,540
Ta -da! Ah.

358
00:20:49,620 --> 00:20:50,820
Here, let me show you how it works.

359
00:20:52,260 --> 00:20:54,760
Oh. Let's see here.

360
00:20:56,240 --> 00:20:58,600
Oh, Jason, they're beautiful.

361
00:20:59,940 --> 00:21:01,260
Thank you, honey.

362
00:21:01,320 --> 00:21:03,360
Thank you. They look real, though.

363
00:21:03,840 --> 00:21:04,500
Oh, they do.

364
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:08,540
Ben, I'm so sorry.

365
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:11,000
There's no gift for you.

366
00:21:11,360 --> 00:21:12,500
Do you want Nick back?

367
00:21:13,020 --> 00:21:15,280
No, Walter. He's yours.

368
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:17,180
Walter, why don't you check the bag?

369
00:21:17,380 --> 00:21:19,000
Maybe Santa left something for Ben.

370
00:21:19,020 --> 00:21:21,000
Yeah. No, it's empty.

371
00:21:21,420 --> 00:21:23,060
Well, why don't you check it anyway?

372
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:24,280
There might be something there.

373
00:21:24,480 --> 00:21:26,260
Yeah. No, really, it's empty.

374
00:21:26,600 --> 00:21:28,100
See, we put the ground beef in the freezer.

375
00:21:28,300 --> 00:21:31,100
Walter, check the bag.

376
00:21:34,660 --> 00:21:37,860
Oh, okay. Yes, I'll take them.

377
00:21:39,860 --> 00:21:45,180
Oh, wow! There is a present for Ben.

378
00:21:45,180 --> 00:21:50,000
There is? Wow, a puppy for me!

379
00:21:50,460 --> 00:21:53,600
Yeah. He looks just like Nick.

380
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:56,660
Well, thank you, all of you, for everything.

381
00:21:58,020 --> 00:21:59,540
Nick is beautiful.

382
00:21:59,840 --> 00:22:01,060
So is my puppy.

383
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:04,020
I think I'll name her...

384
00:22:04,020 --> 00:22:07,880
Walter. Aw. Merry Christmas!

385
00:22:07,880 --> 00:22:22,340
Merry Christmas! Well, Walter, we'll just leave this little snack for Santa Claus,

386
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:29,800
which is not to say that I believe in Santa Claus, but in life, Walter, you'll find

387
00:22:29,800 --> 00:22:31,440
it pays to hedge your bets.

388
00:22:37,180 --> 00:22:38,660
Ho, ho, ho!

389
00:22:38,760 --> 00:23:15,140
Merry Christmas! Thank you.
